;; Package counter implements a simple counter system for collecting
;; totally public telemetry data.
;;
;; There are two kinds of counters, basic counters and stack counters.
;; Basic counters are created by [New].
;; Stack counters are created by [NewStack].
;; Both are incremented by calling Inc().
;;
;; Basic counters are very cheap. Stack counters are more expensive, as they
;; require parsing the stack. (Stack counters are implemented as basic counters
;; whose names are the concatenation of the name and the stack trace. There is
;; an upper limit on the size of this name, about 4K bytes. If the name is too
;; long the stack will be truncated and "truncated" appended.)
;;
;; When counter files expire they are turned into reports by the upload
;; package. The first time any counter file is created for a user, a random day
;; of the week is selected on which counter files will expire. For the first
;; week, that day is more than 7 days (but not more than two weeks) in the
;; future. After that the counter files expire weekly on the same day of the
;; week.
;;
;; # Counter Naming
;;
;; Counter names passed to [New] and [NewStack] should follow these
;; conventions:
;;
;;   - Names cannot contain whitespace or newlines.
;;
;;   - Names must be valid unicode, with no unprintable characters.
;;
;;   - Names may contain at most one ':'. In the counter "foo:bar", we refer to
;;     "foo" as the "chart name" and "bar" as the "bucket name".
;;
;;   - The '/' character should partition counter names into a hierarchy. The
;;     root of this hierarchy should identify the logical entity that "owns"
;;     the counter. This could be an application, such as "gopls" in the case
;;     of "gopls/client:vscode", or a shared library, such as "crash" in the
;;     case of the "crash/crash" counter owned by the crashmonitor library. If
;;     the entity name itself contains a '/', that's ok: "cmd/go/flag" is fine.
;;
;;   - Words should be '-' separated, as in "gopls/completion/errors-latency"
;;
;;   - Histograms should use bucket names identifying upper bounds with '<'.
;;     For example given two counters "gopls/completion/latency:<50ms" and
;;     "gopls/completion/latency:<100ms", the "<100ms" bucket counts events
;;     with latency in the half-open interval [50ms, 100ms).
;;
;; # Debugging
;;
;; The GODEBUG environment variable can enable printing of additional debug
;; information for counters. Adding GODEBUG=countertrace=1 to the environment
;; of a process using counters causes the x/telemetry/counter package to log
;; counter information to stderr.
(in-ns 'go.cmd.vendor.golang_org.x.telemetry.counter) (go/file "doc.go" :doc "Package counter implements a simple counter system for collecting\ntotally public telemetry data.\n\nThere are two kinds of counters, basic counters and stack counters.\nBasic counters are created by [New].\nStack counters are created by [NewStack].\nBoth are incremented by calling Inc().\n\nBasic counters are very cheap. Stack counters are more expensive, as they\nrequire parsing the stack. (Stack counters are implemented as basic counters\nwhose names are the concatenation of the name and the stack trace. There is\nan upper limit on the size of this name, about 4K bytes. If the name is too\nlong the stack will be truncated and \"truncated\" appended.)\n\nWhen counter files expire they are turned into reports by the upload\npackage. The first time any counter file is created for a user, a random day\nof the week is selected on which counter files will expire. For the first\nweek, that day is more than 7 days (but not more than two weeks) in the\nfuture. After that the counter files expire weekly on the same day of the\nweek.\n\n# Counter Naming\n\nCounter names passed to [New] and [NewStack] should follow these\nconventions:\n\n  - Names cannot contain whitespace or newlines.\n\n  - Names must be valid unicode, with no unprintable characters.\n\n  - Names may contain at most one ':'. In the counter \"foo:bar\", we refer to\n    \"foo\" as the \"chart name\" and \"bar\" as the \"bucket name\".\n\n  - The '/' character should partition counter names into a hierarchy. The\n    root of this hierarchy should identify the logical entity that \"owns\"\n    the counter. This could be an application, such as \"gopls\" in the case\n    of \"gopls/client:vscode\", or a shared library, such as \"crash\" in the\n    case of the \"crash/crash\" counter owned by the crashmonitor library. If\n    the entity name itself contains a '/', that's ok: \"cmd/go/flag\" is fine.\n\n  - Words should be '-' separated, as in \"gopls/completion/errors-latency\"\n\n  - Histograms should use bucket names identifying upper bounds with '<'.\n    For example given two counters \"gopls/completion/latency:<50ms\" and\n    \"gopls/completion/latency:<100ms\", the \"<100ms\" bucket counts events\n    with latency in the half-open interval [50ms, 100ms).\n\n# Debugging\n\nThe GODEBUG environment variable can enable printing of additional debug\ninformation for counters. Adding GODEBUG=countertrace=1 to the environment\nof a process using counters causes the x/telemetry/counter package to log\ncounter information to stderr.\n" :imports [])
